 the 18th and 19th centuries, continued to focus on undergraduate education and the development of degree programs"},{"sourceSlug":"Women's rights movement","sourceName":"Women's rights movement","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"bachelors-degree","targetName":"Bachelor's degree","context":"The women's rights movement, which emerged in the 19th century, played a significant role in expanding higher education access to previously excluded groups"},{"sourceSlug":"Working-class movements","sourceName":"Working-class movements","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"bachelors-degree","targetName":"Bachelor's degree","context":"The working-class movements, which emerged in the 19th and 20th centuries, also played a significant role in expanding higher education access to previously excluded groups"},{"sourceSlug":"Social mobility","sourceName":"Social mobility","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"bachelors-degree","targetName":"Bachelor's degree","context":"The concept of social mobility, which emerged in the 19th century, played a significant role in shaping the development and significance of degree programs"},{"sourceSlug":"Intellectual pursuits","sourceName":"Intellectual pursuits","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"bachelors-degree","targetName":"Bachelor's degree","context":"The concept of intellectual pursuits, which emerged in the 19th century, played a significant role in shaping the development and significance of degree programs"}],"places":[{"name":"University of Bologna","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Oxford University","role":"Major battle site"},{"name":"Cambridge University","role":"Continental hub for higher education"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: bachelor's degree","type":"Reference article","slug":"bachelors-degree"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q163727"],"tags":["academic degree","General Religious Events","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"bachelor's degree left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 76 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced subsequent historical developments. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Bachelor's Degree is the foundational first degree of the Western university system — a qualification that emerged in the medieval European universities of the 12th–13th centuries and has become the world's most widely recognised academic credential. The term derives from the Latin 'baccalaureus' (the etymology is disputed — possibly from 'bacca lauri', laurel berry, as a symbol of learning; or from 'baccalarius', a young knight or farm apprentice; or from later folk-etymological conflation). The degree system emerged at the Universities of Bologna, Paris, and Oxford in the 12th–13th centuries as a formal credential distinguishing students who had completed the introductory curriculum of the liberal arts (the trivium: grammar, rhetoric, logic; and quadrivium: arithmetic, geometry, music, astronomy) from those who had progressed to the higher faculties (theology, law, medicine).

In the medieval system, the bachelor's degree was not a terminal qualification but a stage in a cursus studiorum leading to the master's or doctor's degree. A bachelor was licensed to teach under supervision — similar to the position of a journeyman in the craft guilds — before proceeding to full mastership. The degree structure reflected the guild-craft model that medieval universities consciously adopted: master (magister), journeyman (baccalaureus), and apprentice. The disputations, lectures, and examinations that constituted the bachelor's degree programme were designed to train students in formal argumentation — the scholastic method that was the intellectual technology of medieval university education.

The bachelor's degree underwent fundamental transformation in the 19th–20th centuries: from a minority qualification of the learned elite to a mass-market credential that by the early 21st century is held by over a quarter of the adult population in developed countries. This transformation — driven by industrial-era credentialism, the expansion of the state sector, and the 'degree inflation' of post-WWII economic expansion — has made the bachelor's degree simultaneously the global standard of educational attainment and the subject of persistent debates about its value, cost, and accessibility.","subjects":["Higher Education","Medieval History","Academic Credentials","Classical Era","University History","Medieval Era","Education","Intellectual History"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The bachelor's degree has had a profound impact on the development of higher education and intellectual pursuits, shaping social mobility and expanding access to previously excluded groups.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
